'''advantage''' (ad-vȧn′tạ̄j), ''n.'' [< ME. ''avantage'', ''avauntage'', < OF. (and F.) ''avantage'', “an advantage, odds; overplus; addition; eeking; a benefit, furtherance, forwarding,” etc. (Cotgrave), = Pr. ''avantage'' (ML. reflex ''avantagium''), < ML. *''abantaticum'', advantage, < ''abante'', > OF. ''avant'', etc., before: see ''advance'', ''v.''] 1. Any state, condition, circumstance, opportunity, or means specially favorable to success, prosperity, interest, reputation, or any desired end; anything that aids, assists, or is of service: as, he had the ''advantage'' of a good constitution, of an excellent education; the enemy had the ''advantage'' of elevated ground; “the ''advantages'' of a close alliance,” ''Macaulay''.
{{smaller block|''Advantage'' is a better soldier than rashness.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., iii. 6.}}}}
{{smaller block|The streets, seen now under the ''advantages'' of a warm morning sun adding a beauty of its own to whatever it glanced upon, showed much more brilliantly than ours of Rome.
{{right|''W. Ware'', Zenobia, I. 58.}}}}
2. Superiority or prevalence: regularly with ''of'' or ''over''.
{{smaller block|Lest Satan should get an ''advantage of'' us.
{{right|2 Cor. ii. 11.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>I have seen the hungry ocean gain
''Advantage'' on the kingdom of the shore.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, lxiv.}}}}
{{smaller block|The special ''advantage'' of manhood ''over'' youth lies . . . in the sense of reality and limitation.
{{right|''J. R. Seeley'', Nat. Religion, p. 145.}}}}
3. Benefit; gain; profit.
{{smaller block|What ''advantage'' will it be unto thee?
{{right|Job xxxv. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Yet hath Sir Proteus, for that’s his name,
Made use and fair ''advantage'' of his days.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., ii. 4.}}}}

